    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI made the `householdId` deterministic and rewired the household rules around it. Three files changed:\n\n**`HouseholdResolver.java`**\n- `householdId(owner)` now returns `SHA-256(normalizeIdentity(lastName) + \"|\" + postcode)` truncated to the first 12 hex chars \u2014 keyed on **postcode** instead of the normalized address, and with no `HH-` prefix, exactly as the spec defines it. A null postcode is treated as empty string.\n- `householdExists(owner)` now compares the *computed* household id against existing owners (same lastName + postcode \u21d2 same household), and I dropped the now-unused `AddressNormalizer` dependency.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`**\n- The household id is now assigned **unconditionally** (`owner.setHouseholdId(householdResolver.householdId(owner))`) before the duplicate checks, so both duplicate detection (via `identityKey`, which folds in `householdId`) and the household rules key off the same computed value.\n- Added the **household-duplicate block**: if an existing owner already occupies the candidate's household and the request does *not* set `sharesHousehold`, it returns **409**. `sharesHousehold` now only *bypasses* this block \u2014 it no longer creates the link (the deterministic id does that on its own).\n- The existing identity-key hard-duplicate check remains, so a truly identical owner (same phone/email/household) is still rejected even with `sharesHousehold`.\n- A **declared household member** (`sharesHousehold` + existing household) is no longer flagged as a possible duplicate (`possibleDuplicateOf` is skipped for it), per \"a declared member is not a suspected duplicate.\"\n\n`membershipLevel` needed no change: it derives from email presence and `namesakeCount`, not household size, so there was no household-keyed input there to redirect.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreSameLastNameAndPostcodeShareHouseholdId` passes: two owners with the same lastName+postcode share a household id (the second created via `sharesHousehold`), and a different lastName yields a different id. Full suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.\n\nOne deliberate call worth flagging: the spec says householdId *is* \"the first 12 hex characters,\" so I dropped the previous `HH-` prefix. The only test asserts the observable contract (same-in/same-out, different-in/different-out) rather than the literal format, so this is safe; the `openapi.yml` example string still shows the old `HH-\u2026` form (docs only) if you'd prefer I update that too.",
